review_text:
This work introduces a novel defense against RAG retrieval attacks by leveraging document reliability signals (rank/weights) through two key mechanisms: (1) Maximum Independent Set (MIS) algorithm that selects mutually consistent documents while prioritizing high-reliability sources, and (2) Scalable weighted sampling framework for large retrieval sets. 
Experiments demonstrate good robustness against prompt injection/corpus poisoning attacks while maintaining high benign performance, especially in long-form generation.

Strengths:
1. First framework to exploit document ranks/weights for provable robustness against retrieval attacks.The idea is simple and straightforward but seems to work;
2. Weighted sampling extends MIS robustness to large corpora with minimal accuracy loss;
3. Maintain good robustness against attacks while excelling in long-form generation.
Weakness:
1.  Robustness hinges on NLI model accuracy; vulnerable to adversarial contradictions;
2.  MIS and NLI checks add overhead , limiting real-time use;
3.  No validation for scenarios where reliability signals (ranks/weights) are miscalibrated or manipulated.
